<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxerxes III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">assassinated <a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artaxerxes III was an Achaemenid king who reigned from 358 BCE until he was assassinated in 338 BCE. He suppressed revolts and reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 343 BCE, restoring royal authority. His murder by his official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagoas">Bagoas</a> brought instability shortly before Alexander's invasion, contributing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>'s decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii.jpg" alt="Artaxerxes III" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AR VLD · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_relief_of_Artaxerxes_III_in_Persepolis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Greek Wars, a peripheral embarrassment from the Persian perspective, but by the loss of Egypt in 404 BCE, when the province revolted successfully at the death of Darius II and remained independent under native dynasties for some six decades until <b>Artaxerxes III</b> reconquered it in 343 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the Persian era, in 351 BCE, <b>Artaxerxes III</b> invaded Phoenicia, and in 333 BCE Alexander the Great occupied the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 351 BCE Sidon, under its king Tennes, led a revolt against the Persian king <b>Artaxerxes III</b>; when the Persian army approached, Tennes betrayed the city, and the Sidonians, Diodorus says, set fire to it rather than surrender.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Kingship could be dangerous: Xerxes I was murdered in his bed, and <b>Artaxerxes III</b>, Greek writers say, was poisoned by the eunuch Bagoas in 338 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In 343 BCE <b>Artaxerxes III</b> defeated Nectanebo II at Pelusium.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirtieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>After failed attempts in 351 BCE, the Persian king <b>Artaxerxes III</b> invaded Egypt in 343 BCE, defeating Nectanebo II of the Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt at Pelusium and capturing Memphis.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
